title: Quick Start
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Table is a headless table library. It manages your table's state and logic (sorting, filtering, pagination, selection, and more) while you keep 100% control over the markup and styles. This page gets you from install to a rendering Lit table, then shows how to layer on your first feature."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"installation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Installation"}]},{"type":"code","value":"npm install @tanstack/lit-table","lang":"bash"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"your-first-table","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Your First Table"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The component below is complete. Paste it into a Lit app and you will see a working table. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/lit-table"},{"type":"text","value":" adapter is built around "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TableController"},{"type":"text","value":", a Lit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ReactiveController"},{"type":"text","value":" that constructs the table once, subscribes the host to state changes, and gives you a fresh table instance on each render pass."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { LitElement, html } from 'lit'\nimport { customElement, state } from 'lit/decorators.js'\nimport { repeat } from 'lit/directives/repeat.js'\nimport { FlexRender, TableController, tableFeatures } from '@tanstack/lit-table'\nimport type { ColumnDef } from '@tanstack/lit-table'\n\n// 1. Define the shape of your data\ntype Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n}\n\n// 2. New in v9: declare which features this table uses (none yet)\nconst features = tableFeatures({})\n\n// 3. Define your columns\nconst columns: Array<ColumnDef<typeof features, Person>> = [\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'firstName', // accessorKey shorthand\n    header: 'First Name',\n    cell: (info) => info.getValue(),\n  },\n  {\n    accessorFn: (row) => row.lastName, // accessorFn alternative with a custom id\n    id: 'lastName',\n    header: () => html`<span>Last Name</span>`,\n    cell: (info) => html`<i>${info.getValue<string>()}</i>`,\n  },\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'age',\n    header: () => 'Age',\n  },\n]\n\n@customElement('person-table')\nexport class PersonTable extends LitElement {\n  // 4. Store your data in a reactive property\n  @state()\n  private data: Array<Person> = [\n    { firstName: 'tanner', lastName: 'linsley', age: 24 },\n    { firstName: 'tandy', lastName: 'miller', age: 40 },\n    { firstName: 'joe', lastName: 'dirte', age: 45 },\n  ]\n\n  // 5. Create one TableController for the host element\n  private tableController = new TableController<typeof features, Person>(this)\n\n  protected render() {\n    // 6. Create the table instance during render\n    const table = this.tableController.table(\n      {\n        features,\n        columns,\n        data: this.data,\n      },\n      () => ({}), // state selector, empty since we use no feature state yet\n    )\n\n    // 7. Render markup from the table instance APIs\n    return html`\n      <table>\n        <thead>\n          ${repeat(\n            table.getHeaderGroups(),\n            (headerGroup) => headerGroup.id,\n            (headerGroup) => html`\n              <tr>\n                ${repeat(\n                  headerGroup.headers,\n                  (header) => header.id,\n                  (header) => html`\n                    <th>\n                      ${header.isPlaceholder ? null : FlexRender({ header })}\n                    </th>\n                  `,\n                )}\n              </tr>\n            `,\n          )}\n        </thead>\n        <tbody>\n          ${repeat(\n            table.getRowModel().rows,\n            (row) => row.id,\n            (row) => html`\n              <tr>\n                ${repeat(\n                  row.getAllCells(),\n                  (cell) => cell.id,\n                  (cell) => html`<td>${FlexRender({ cell })}</td>`,\n                )}\n              </tr>\n            `,\n          )}\n        </tbody>\n      </table>\n    `\n  }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A few things to note:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures({})"},{"type":"text","value":" declares which optional features the table uses. Registering only what you need keeps bundles small and gives TypeScript accurate types for the table instance."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The core row model is always included automatically. Feature row models (sorting, filtering, pagination) are registered as slots directly on the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures({...})"},{"type":"text","value":" call when you need them."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"FlexRender"},{"type":"text","value":" renders the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"header"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"footer"},{"type":"text","value":" definitions from your columns, whether they are plain values or Lit templates. It is also attached to the instance as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.FlexRender"},{"type":"text","value":" if you prefer not to import it."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The second argument to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableController.table(...)"},{"type":"text","value":" is a state selector. It controls what "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.state"},{"type":"text","value":" contains; an empty selector is fine until you use feature state."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the full "},{"type":"link","href":"./examples/basic-table-controller","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic TableController example"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for a runnable version with more columns and a footer."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"add-a-feature-sorting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add a Feature: Sorting"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Features are opt-in in v9. To make columns sortable, register "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowSortingFeature"},{"type":"text","value":" and the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortedRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" factory in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":", then wire the header click handler."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  FlexRender,\n  TableController,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortFns,\n  tableFeatures,\n} from '@tanstack/lit-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature, // enables sorting APIs and state\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(), // client-side sorting\n  sortFns,\n})\n\n@customElement('person-table')\nexport class PersonTable extends LitElement {\n  // data and tableController unchanged from above\n\n  protected render() {\n    const table = this.tableController.table(\n      {\n        features,\n        columns,\n        data: this.data,\n      },\n      (state) => ({ sorting: state.sorting }), // select the sorting state\n    )\n\n    return html`\n      <table>\n        <thead>\n          ${repeat(\n            table.getHeaderGroups(),\n            (headerGroup) => headerGroup.id,\n            (headerGroup) => html`\n              <tr>\n                ${repeat(\n                  headerGroup.headers,\n                  (header) => header.id,\n                  (header) => html`\n                    <th>\n                      ${\n                        header.isPlaceholder\n                          ? null\n                          : html`<div\n                              @click=${header.column.getToggleSortingHandler()}\n                              style=\"cursor: ${\n                                header.column.getCanSort()\n                                  ? 'pointer'\n                                  : 'default'\n                              }\"\n                            >\n                              ${FlexRender({ header })}\n                              ${\n                                { asc: ' 🔼', desc: ' 🔽' }[\n                                  header.column.getIsSorted() as string\n                                ] ?? null\n                              }\n                            </div>`\n                      }\n                    </th>\n                  `,\n                )}\n              </tr>\n            `,\n          )}\n        </thead>\n        <!-- tbody unchanged from above -->\n      </table>\n    `\n  }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Clicking a header now toggles between ascending, descending, and unsorted. Every other feature follows this same pattern: register the feature (and its row model factory as a slot on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":" if it has one), then use the APIs it adds to the table, columns, and rows. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/sorting.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sorting Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and the "},{"type":"link","href":"./examples/sorting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sorting example"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for custom sort functions, multi-sorting, and per-column options."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"where-to-go-next","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Where to Go Next"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table state."}]},{"type":"text","value":" In v9, table state is backed by TanStack Store atoms. You usually do not need to manage it yourself. Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":" for starting values and call feature APIs like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.setSorting(...)"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.nextPage()"},{"type":"text","value":". When your app should own a state slice (via the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atoms"},{"type":"text","value":" option), or you want fine-grained subscriptions, read the "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/table-state.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table State Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":". It is the foundational guide for everything else."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Feature guides."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Each feature has its own guide, such as "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/column-filtering.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Filtering"}]},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/pagination.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pagination"}]},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/row-selection.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row Selection"}]},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/column-visibility.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Visibility"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Composable tables."}]},{"type":"text","value":" When multiple tables in your app share features, row models, and component conventions, define them once with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHook"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns,\n})\n\nconst { useAppTable, createAppColumnHelper } = createTableHook({ features })","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Then call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useAppTable(this, { columns, data })"},{"type":"text","value":" from your components instead of managing a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TableController"},{"type":"text","value":" directly. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/composable-tables.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Composable Tables Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the full pattern, including pre-bound cell and header components."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Examples."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Browse the runnable "},{"type":"link","href":"./examples/basic-table-controller","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lit examples"}]},{"type":"text","value":", from basic tables to feature demos, to see intended usage end to end."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"installation","text":"Installation","level":2},{"id":"your-first-table","text":"Your First Table","level":2},{"id":"add-a-feature-sorting","text":"Add a Feature: Sorting","level":2},{"id":"where-to-go-next","text":"Where to Go Next","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nTanStack Table is a headless table library. It manages your table's state and logic (sorting, filtering, pagination, selection, and more) while you keep 100% control over the markup and styles. This page gets you from install to a rendering Lit table, then shows how to layer on your first feature.\n\n## Installation\n\n```bash\nnpm install @tanstack/lit-table\n```\n\n## Your First Table\n\nThe component below is complete. Paste it into a Lit app and you will see a working table. The `@tanstack/lit-table` adapter is built around `TableController`, a Lit `ReactiveController` that constructs the table once, subscribes the host to state changes, and gives you a fresh table instance on each render pass.\n\n```ts\nimport { LitElement, html } from 'lit'\nimport { customElement, state } from 'lit/decorators.js'\nimport { repeat } from 'lit/directives/repeat.js'\nimport { FlexRender, TableController, tableFeatures } from '@tanstack/lit-table'\nimport type { ColumnDef } from '@tanstack/lit-table'\n\n// 1. Define the shape of your data\ntype Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n}\n\n// 2. New in v9: declare which features this table uses (none yet)\nconst features = tableFeatures({})\n\n// 3. Define your columns\nconst columns: Array<ColumnDef<typeof features, Person>> = [\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'firstName', // accessorKey shorthand\n    header: 'First Name',\n    cell: (info) => info.getValue(),\n  },\n  {\n    accessorFn: (row) => row.lastName, // accessorFn alternative with a custom id\n    id: 'lastName',\n    header: () => html`<span>Last Name</span>`,\n    cell: (info) => html`<i>${info.getValue<string>()}</i>`,\n  },\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'age',\n    header: () => 'Age',\n  },\n]\n\n@customElement('person-table')\nexport class PersonTable extends LitElement {\n  // 4. Store your data in a reactive property\n  @state()\n  private data: Array<Person> = [\n    { firstName: 'tanner', lastName: 'linsley', age: 24 },\n    { firstName: 'tandy', lastName: 'miller', age: 40 },\n    { firstName: 'joe', lastName: 'dirte', age: 45 },\n  ]\n\n  // 5. Create one TableController for the host element\n  private tableController = new TableController<typeof features, Person>(this)\n\n  protected render() {\n    // 6. Create the table instance during render\n    const table = this.tableController.table(\n      {\n        features,\n        columns,\n        data: this.data,\n      },\n      () => ({}), // state selector, empty since we use no feature state yet\n    )\n\n    // 7. Render markup from the table instance APIs\n    return html`\n      <table>\n        <thead>\n          ${repeat(\n            table.getHeaderGroups(),\n            (headerGroup) => headerGroup.id,\n            (headerGroup) => html`\n              <tr>\n                ${repeat(\n                  headerGroup.headers,\n                  (header) => header.id,\n                  (header) => html`\n                    <th>\n                      ${header.isPlaceholder ? null : FlexRender({ header })}\n                    </th>\n                  `,\n                )}\n              </tr>\n            `,\n          )}\n        </thead>\n        <tbody>\n          ${repeat(\n            table.getRowModel().rows,\n            (row) => row.id,\n            (row) => html`\n              <tr>\n                ${repeat(\n                  row.getAllCells(),\n                  (cell) => cell.id,\n                  (cell) => html`<td>${FlexRender({ cell })}</td>`,\n                )}\n              </tr>\n            `,\n          )}\n        </tbody>\n      </table>\n    `\n  }\n}\n```\n\nA few things to note:\n\n- `tableFeatures({})` declares which optional features the table uses. Registering only what you need keeps bundles small and gives TypeScript accurate types for the table instance.\n- The core row model is always included automatically. Feature row models (sorting, filtering, pagination) are registered as slots directly on the `tableFeatures({...})` call when you need them.\n- `FlexRender` renders the `header`, `cell`, and `footer` definitions from your columns, whether they are plain values or Lit templates. It is also attached to the instance as `table.FlexRender` if you prefer not to import it.\n- The second argument to `tableController.table(...)` is a state selector. It controls what `table.state` contains; an empty selector is fine until you use feature state.\n\nSee the full [Basic TableController example](./examples/basic-table-controller) for a runnable version with more columns and a footer.\n\n## Add a Feature: Sorting\n\nFeatures are opt-in in v9. To make columns sortable, register `rowSortingFeature` and the `sortedRowModel` factory in `tableFeatures`, then wire the header click handler.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  FlexRender,\n  TableController,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortFns,\n  tableFeatures,\n} from '@tanstack/lit-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature, // enables sorting APIs and state\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(), // client-side sorting\n  sortFns,\n})\n\n@customElement('person-table')\nexport class PersonTable extends LitElement {\n  // data and tableController unchanged from above\n\n  protected render() {\n    const table = this.tableController.table(\n      {\n        features,\n        columns,\n        data: this.data,\n      },\n      (state) => ({ sorting: state.sorting }), // select the sorting state\n    )\n\n    return html`\n      <table>\n        <thead>\n          ${repeat(\n            table.getHeaderGroups(),\n            (headerGroup) => headerGroup.id,\n            (headerGroup) => html`\n              <tr>\n                ${repeat(\n                  headerGroup.headers,\n                  (header) => header.id,\n                  (header) => html`\n                    <th>\n                      ${\n                        header.isPlaceholder\n                          ? null\n                          : html`<div\n                              @click=${header.column.getToggleSortingHandler()}\n                              style=\"cursor: ${\n                                header.column.getCanSort()\n                                  ? 'pointer'\n                                  : 'default'\n                              }\"\n                            >\n                              ${FlexRender({ header })}\n                              ${\n                                { asc: ' 🔼', desc: ' 🔽' }[\n                                  header.column.getIsSorted() as string\n                                ] ?? null\n                              }\n                            </div>`\n                      }\n                    </th>\n                  `,\n                )}\n              </tr>\n            `,\n          )}\n        </thead>\n        <!-- tbody unchanged from above -->\n      </table>\n    `\n  }\n}\n```\n\nClicking a header now toggles between ascending, descending, and unsorted. Every other feature follows this same pattern: register the feature (and its row model factory as a slot on `tableFeatures` if it has one), then use the APIs it adds to the table, columns, and rows. See the [Sorting Guide](./guide/sorting.md) and the [Sorting example](./examples/sorting) for custom sort functions, multi-sorting, and per-column options.\n\n## Where to Go Next\n\n**Table state.** In v9, table state is backed by TanStack Store atoms. You usually do not need to manage it yourself. Set `initialState` for starting values and call feature APIs like `table.setSorting(...)` or `table.nextPage()`. When your app should own a state slice (via the `atoms` option), or you want fine-grained subscriptions, read the [Table State Guide](./guide/table-state.md). It is the foundational guide for everything else.\n\n**Feature guides.** Each feature has its own guide, such as [Column Filtering](./guide/column-filtering.md), [Pagination](./guide/pagination.md), [Row Selection](./guide/row-selection.md), and [Column Visibility](./guide/column-visibility.md).\n\n**Composable tables.** When multiple tables in your app share features, row models, and component conventions, define them once with `createTableHook`:\n\n```ts\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns,\n})\n\nconst { useAppTable, createAppColumnHelper } = createTableHook({ features })\n```\n\nThen call `useAppTable(this, { columns, data })` from your components instead of managing a `TableController` directly. See the [Composable Tables Guide](./guide/composable-tables.md) for the full pattern, including pre-bound cell and header components.\n\n**Examples.** Browse the runnable [Lit examples](./examples/basic-table-controller), from basic tables to feature demos, to see intended usage end to end.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Quick Start\n---\n\nTanStack Table is a headless table library. It manages your table's state and logic (sorting, filtering, pagination, selection, and more) while you keep 100% control over the markup and styles. This page gets you from install to a rendering Lit table, then shows how to layer on your first feature.\n\n## Installation\n\n```bash\nnpm install @tanstack/lit-table\n```\n\n## Your First Table\n\nThe component below is complete. Paste it into a Lit app and you will see a working table. The `@tanstack/lit-table` adapter is built around `TableController`, a Lit `ReactiveController` that constructs the table once, subscribes the host to state changes, and gives you a fresh table instance on each render pass.\n\n```ts\nimport { LitElement, html } from 'lit'\nimport { customElement, state } from 'lit/decorators.js'\nimport { repeat } from 'lit/directives/repeat.js'\nimport { FlexRender, TableController, tableFeatures } from '@tanstack/lit-table'\nimport type { ColumnDef } from '@tanstack/lit-table'\n\n// 1. Define the shape of your data\ntype Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n}\n\n// 2. New in v9: declare which features this table uses (none yet)\nconst features = tableFeatures({})\n\n// 3. Define your columns\nconst columns: Array<ColumnDef<typeof features, Person>> = [\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'firstName', // accessorKey shorthand\n    header: 'First Name',\n    cell: (info) => info.getValue(),\n  },\n  {\n    accessorFn: (row) => row.lastName, // accessorFn alternative with a custom id\n    id: 'lastName',\n    header: () => html`<span>Last Name</span>`,\n    cell: (info) => html`<i>${info.getValue<string>()}</i>`,\n  },\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'age',\n    header: () => 'Age',\n  },\n]\n\n@customElement('person-table')\nexport class PersonTable extends LitElement {\n  // 4. Store your data in a reactive property\n  @state()\n  private data: Array<Person> = [\n    { firstName: 'tanner', lastName: 'linsley', age: 24 },\n    { firstName: 'tandy', lastName: 'miller', age: 40 },\n    { firstName: 'joe', lastName: 'dirte', age: 45 },\n  ]\n\n  // 5. Create one TableController for the host element\n  private tableController = new TableController<typeof features, Person>(this)\n\n  protected render() {\n    // 6. Create the table instance during render\n    const table = this.tableController.table(\n      {\n        features,\n        columns,\n        data: this.data,\n      },\n      () => ({}), // state selector, empty since we use no feature state yet\n    )\n\n    // 7. Render markup from the table instance APIs\n    return html`\n      <table>\n        <thead>\n          ${repeat(\n            table.getHeaderGroups(),\n            (headerGroup) => headerGroup.id,\n            (headerGroup) => html`\n              <tr>\n                ${repeat(\n                  headerGroup.headers,\n                  (header) => header.id,\n                  (header) => html`\n                    <th>\n                      ${header.isPlaceholder ? null : FlexRender({ header })}\n                    </th>\n                  `,\n                )}\n              </tr>\n            `,\n          )}\n        </thead>\n        <tbody>\n          ${repeat(\n            table.getRowModel().rows,\n            (row) => row.id,\n            (row) => html`\n              <tr>\n                ${repeat(\n                  row.getAllCells(),\n                  (cell) => cell.id,\n                  (cell) => html`<td>${FlexRender({ cell })}</td>`,\n                )}\n              </tr>\n            `,\n          )}\n        </tbody>\n      </table>\n    `\n  }\n}\n```\n\nA few things to note:\n\n- `tableFeatures({})` declares which optional features the table uses. Registering only what you need keeps bundles small and gives TypeScript accurate types for the table instance.\n- The core row model is always included automatically. Feature row models (sorting, filtering, pagination) are registered as slots directly on the `tableFeatures({...})` call when you need them.\n- `FlexRender` renders the `header`, `cell`, and `footer` definitions from your columns, whether they are plain values or Lit templates. It is also attached to the instance as `table.FlexRender` if you prefer not to import it.\n- The second argument to `tableController.table(...)` is a state selector. It controls what `table.state` contains; an empty selector is fine until you use feature state.\n\nSee the full [Basic TableController example](./examples/basic-table-controller) for a runnable version with more columns and a footer.\n\n## Add a Feature: Sorting\n\nFeatures are opt-in in v9. To make columns sortable, register `rowSortingFeature` and the `sortedRowModel` factory in `tableFeatures`, then wire the header click handler.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  FlexRender,\n  TableController,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortFns,\n  tableFeatures,\n} from '@tanstack/lit-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature, // enables sorting APIs and state\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(), // client-side sorting\n  sortFns,\n})\n\n@customElement('person-table')\nexport class PersonTable extends LitElement {\n  // data and tableController unchanged from above\n\n  protected render() {\n    const table = this.tableController.table(\n      {\n        features,\n        columns,\n        data: this.data,\n      },\n      (state) => ({ sorting: state.sorting }), // select the sorting state\n    )\n\n    return html`\n      <table>\n        <thead>\n          ${repeat(\n            table.getHeaderGroups(),\n            (headerGroup) => headerGroup.id,\n            (headerGroup) => html`\n              <tr>\n                ${repeat(\n                  headerGroup.headers,\n                  (header) => header.id,\n                  (header) => html`\n                    <th>\n                      ${\n                        header.isPlaceholder\n                          ? null\n                          : html`<div\n                              @click=${header.column.getToggleSortingHandler()}\n                              style=\"cursor: ${\n                                header.column.getCanSort()\n                                  ? 'pointer'\n                                  : 'default'\n                              }\"\n                            >\n                              ${FlexRender({ header })}\n                              ${\n                                { asc: ' 🔼', desc: ' 🔽' }[\n                                  header.column.getIsSorted() as string\n                                ] ?? null\n                              }\n                            </div>`\n                      }\n                    </th>\n                  `,\n                )}\n              </tr>\n            `,\n          )}\n        </thead>\n        <!-- tbody unchanged from above -->\n      </table>\n    `\n  }\n}\n```\n\nClicking a header now toggles between ascending, descending, and unsorted. Every other feature follows this same pattern: register the feature (and its row model factory as a slot on `tableFeatures` if it has one), then use the APIs it adds to the table, columns, and rows. See the [Sorting Guide](./guide/sorting.md) and the [Sorting example](./examples/sorting) for custom sort functions, multi-sorting, and per-column options.\n\n## Where to Go Next\n\n**Table state.** In v9, table state is backed by TanStack Store atoms. You usually do not need to manage it yourself. Set `initialState` for starting values and call feature APIs like `table.setSorting(...)` or `table.nextPage()`. When your app should own a state slice (via the `atoms` option), or you want fine-grained subscriptions, read the [Table State Guide](./guide/table-state.md). It is the foundational guide for everything else.\n\n**Feature guides.** Each feature has its own guide, such as [Column Filtering](./guide/column-filtering.md), [Pagination](./guide/pagination.md), [Row Selection](./guide/row-selection.md), and [Column Visibility](./guide/column-visibility.md).\n\n**Composable tables.** When multiple tables in your app share features, row models, and component conventions, define them once with `createTableHook`:\n\n```ts\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns,\n})\n\nconst { useAppTable, createAppColumnHelper } = createTableHook({ features })\n```\n\nThen call `useAppTable(this, { columns, data })` from your components instead of managing a `TableController` directly. See the [Composable Tables Guide](./guide/composable-tables.md) for the full pattern, including pre-bound cell and header components.\n\n**Examples.** Browse the runnable [Lit examples](./examples/basic-table-controller), from basic tables to feature demos, to see intended usage end to end.\n"}
